llvm-4.0.0 backport patch: MachineSink splits no critical edge by branch probability (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION for a cheap instruction (a constant,
a zero, a copy) whose value is live on one edge of a conditional branch
only. r284757 (f03f51555a63, 2016-10-20, "Using branch probability to
guide critical edge splitting.", D24818) added to
MachineSinking::isWorthBreakingCriticalEdge a test that breaks the edge
whenever its probability is at or under machine-sink-split-probability-
threshold (40%); a null or `== 0` test gives that edge 37.5%, so 4.0.0
sinks a return's constant onto its own block (`je L; ...; ret; L: mov
eax, 1; ret`). It is not in 3.9.1's MachineSink.cpp; first released in
4.0.0. The game region keeps the constant before the test on one shared
return: 34 such sites at a shared `ret` and none of 4.0.0's form where
the rule alone makes it (round 128A). With this patch 0xc11e40 and
0x7790c0 compile to the base's bytes and no matched game row changes.
The hidden option -machine-sink-split-probability-threshold=0 gives the
same objects.

--- a/lib/CodeGen/MachineSink.cpp
+++ b/lib/CodeGen/MachineSink.cpp
@@ -397,10 +397,6 @@
   if (!MI.isCopy() && !TII->isAsCheapAsAMove(MI))
     return true;
 
-  if (From->isSuccessor(To) && MBPI->getEdgeProbability(From, To) <=
-      BranchProbability(SplitEdgeProbabilityThreshold, 100))
-    return true;
-
   // MI is cheap, we probably don't want to break the critical edge for it.
   // However, if this would allow some definitions of its source operands
   // to be sunk then it's probably worth it.
